 { q: 'Why does vector matter for embroidery?', a: 'Vector artwork scales without quality loss. The digitizer can resize your logo to fit chest, sleeve, or back placements without losing crispness. Raster images (JPG, PNG) become pixelated when scaled up, which forces the digitizer to redraw — adding time, cost, and the risk of subtle differences from your original brand mark.' },
 { q: 'What\'s the difference between AI, EPS, and SVG?', a: 'All three can contain vector artwork. .ai is Adobe Illustrator\'s native format. .eps supports broad design-software compatibility. .svg is web-native vector. The production partner confirms which file formats and elements its workflow supports.' },
 { q: 'Can you embroider from a JPG or PNG?', a: 'A production partner may accept a JPG or PNG. Low-resolution artwork may require redrawing or added setup. Send the highest-resolution file available, then confirm artwork and fee terms in the quote.' },
 { q: 'What logo elements need review for embroidery?', a: 'Fine lines, small text, gradients, effects, color changes, and dense details may require adjustment. The exact artwork, size, garment, backing, machine, digitizing, and production partner determine viability.' },
 { q: 'How do I prep my logo for embroidery?', a: 'Send the highest-quality source artwork available, identify required text and colors, specify the intended garment and placement, and ask the production partner to confirm any simplification, redraw, or file fee before approval.' },
 { q: 'How many colors should my embroidered logo have?', a: 'There is no universal color limit for every design. The production partner must review the exact artwork, size, garment, thread plan, machine, and quote.' },
 { q: 'What size should my embroidered logo be?', a: 'Placement dimensions depend on the exact garment, artwork, wearer size range, hooping, digitizing, and production partner. Approve the final dimensions in the proof or sample.' },
]

