 <aside class="mb-10 border border-hairline bg-panel p-7">
 <p class="mb-3 font-mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-700">Quick Answer</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal sm:text-xl speakable-answer">Send the highest-quality vector artwork available. Common formats include .ai, .eps, .pdf, and .svg. A low-resolution JPG or PNG may require redraw work. Confirm supported formats, minimum detail, colors, proofing, and artwork fees with the production partner.</p>
 </aside>

 <h2 id="why-vector" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Why Vector Matters</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Embroidery starts with digitizing — converting your logo into a stitch file the embroidery machine can read. The digitizer needs the cleanest possible input. Vector files (built from mathematical paths) scale infinitely without losing crispness. Raster files (built from fixed pixels) lose quality when enlarged.</p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">A low-resolution JPG may lack enough detail for clean digitizing. The production partner may request better artwork or quote redraw work. A usable vector file can reduce that preparation.</p>

 <h2 id="file-formats" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Acceptable File Formats</h2>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>.ai (Adobe Illustrator)</strong> — best input. Preserves layers, fonts, effects, swatches. Most digitizers prefer this.</li>
 <li><strong>.eps</strong> — universal vector format. Cleanest cross-software compatibility.</li>
 <li><strong>.pdf</strong> — works if the PDF was exported with vector elements (not flattened to raster).</li>
 <li><strong>.svg</strong> — web-native vector. Works for most digitizers.</li>
 <li><strong>.cdr (CorelDRAW)</strong> — accepted by some shops, but less universal.</li>
 <li><strong>.jpg / .png / .tiff</strong>: raster formats. Acceptance and redraw needs depend on resolution, intended size, and the production workflow.</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">For deeper background see our piece on <a href="/blog/logo-digitizing-101-embroidery/" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">logo digitizing 101</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="design-elements" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Design Elements That Break in Embroidery</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Some logo elements that work great in print don't translate to thread:</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Fine lines</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Ask the digitizer to review fine lines on the exact artwork, size, garment, backing, machine, and placement. Any proposed adjustment should appear in the approved proof or sample.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Very small text</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Text viability depends on the font, size, garment, backing, digitizing, machine, and placement. Approve the final treatment in the proof or sample.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Gradients and color blends</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Gradients are continuous color blends that don't exist in solid-thread embroidery. The digitizer either approximates with banded thread color transitions (works but looks different from print) or simplifies to solid colors. Logos designed for print often need gradient simplification before embroidery.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Drop shadows, glows, transparencies</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">These visual effects exist in print/digital but don't translate to thread. Either remove them before sending the file, or accept that the digitizer will simplify or omit.</p>

 <h2 id="prep-checklist" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Logo Prep Checklist</h2>
 <ol class="mb-6 list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Find the original vector file.</strong> Whoever designed your logo should have an .ai or .eps source file. Track that down before sending a JPG.</li>
 <li><strong>Convert text to outlines.</strong> In Illustrator: select text → Type → Create Outlines. This eliminates font availability issues on the digitizer's end.</li>
 <li><strong>Review fine detail.</strong> Ask the digitizer which lines, text, and effects need adjustment for the exact artwork, size, garment, and machine.</li>
 <li><strong>Review the color plan.</strong> Ask the production partner to confirm thread choices and color changes for the approved design.</li>
 <li><strong>Include Pantone color references.</strong> Specify the exact PMS or Madeira/Robison-Anton thread colors you want matched.</li>
 <li><strong>Specify intended size.</strong> "3.5 inches wide for left-chest" tells the digitizer to optimize density for that scale.</li>
 <li><strong>Note any non-negotiables.</strong> If the brand mark MUST be a specific color or element MUST be visible, flag it explicitly.</li>
 </ol>

 <h2 id="what-digitizer-does" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">What the Digitizer Does With Your File</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Once you send the prepped vector file, the digitizer:</p>
 <ol class="mb-6 list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li>Imports your vector into digitizing software (Wilcom, Pulse, Embird, etc.)</li>
 <li>Manually traces each element, deciding stitch direction, density, type (satin, fill, run)</li>
 <li>Sets stitch order — what gets stitched first, what stops/starts, color sequence</li>
 <li>Adjusts compensation for fabric pull (knit fabrics distort differently than wovens)</li>
 <li>Outputs a stitch file (.dst, .emb, .pes, .jef depending on machine)</li>
 <li>Provides the proof or sew-out step stated in the approved production workflow</li>
 </ol>

 <h2 id="cost" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">What Digitizing Costs</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Digitizing fees depend on the logo and production partner. Color count, stitch count, fine detail, and redraw work can affect cost. Confirm file ownership, retention, reuse, and repeat-order fees in the quote.</p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">For full pricing context see <a href="/blog/how-much-to-embroider-a-shirt/" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">how much it costs to embroider a shirt</a>.</p>

